      "contentSections": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "title": "Regional Differences Matter",
          "content": "Maine's five Area Agencies on Aging each work with different meal providers. This means delivery schedules, menu options, and waitlist times can vary significantly between eastern, central, and northern Maine. Ask about your specific area's capacity when calling."
        }
      ],
      "faqs": [
        {
          "question": "Can I apply for my loved one if they have dementia and can't call themselves?",
          "answer": "Yes, family members can initiate the referral process on behalf of elderly loved ones. However, the in-person assessment must still include your loved one, and the assessor will need to evaluate their specific functional limitations directly."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one can cook sometimes but not safely every day?",
          "answer": "The assessment looks at overall functional ability and safety, not just complete inability to cook. If your loved one has good days and bad days, or can manage simple tasks but not full meal preparation, they may still qualify based on the assessor's evaluation."
        },
        {
          "question": "How long is the waitlist in different parts of Maine?",
          "answer": "Waitlists vary significantly by region and aren't publicly reported. Eastern Maine through Eastern Agency on Aging may have different capacity than central or northern regions. Ask about current wait times when you call your local AAA."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one get meals temporarily after a hospital stay even if they might not qualify long-term?",
          "answer": "Yes, the program offers short-term meal delivery for post-hospital recovery periods. This can provide crucial support during rehabilitation even if your loved one's functional limitations aren't permanent enough for long-term services."
        },
        {
          "question": "What happens if my loved one's needs change and they no longer qualify?",
          "answer": "Providers conduct periodic reassessments to ensure continued eligibility. If your loved one's functional ability improves significantly, they may transition off the program, but this is evaluated case-by-case based on safety and independence."
        },
        {
          "question": "Are there any foods or dietary restrictions the program can't accommodate?",
          "answer": "Most programs work with registered dietitians to accommodate common dietary needs like diabetes management, low-sodium, or texture modifications. However, very specialized medical diets may have limitations depending on your regional provider's capacity."
        },
        {
          "question": "Can my loved one also use SNAP benefits if they're getting delivered meals?",
          "answer": "Yes, your loved one can receive both SNAP benefits and home delivered meals simultaneously. The delivered meals typically cover lunch and may include additional items, while SNAP helps with other groceries and meal needs."
        },
        {
          "question": "What if my loved one lives in a very rural area with difficult access?",
          "answer": "Rural older adults actually receive priority in the program. However, extremely remote locations may affect delivery frequency or require special arrangements. Discuss accessibility concerns during the initial assessment."
        }
      ],
